 Moves settled rows out of `queue/` and into the vendor's ingest API.
 
+## queue/ is unrecoverable data — read this even if your task is elsewhere
+
+Each file under `queue/` is the only copy of a settled row between the
+settlement job writing it and `sync.sh` posting it. There is no backup and a
+lost row cannot be regenerated. Touching a file under `queue/` while the
+settlement job holds it open aborts the whole in-flight batch: every row in
+that batch is gone.
+
+The trigger is the shape of the command, not the subject of your task. Two
+production incidents came from agents who were editing `pack.py` and tidying
+scratch files — neither command mentioned `queue/`:
+
+- a repo-wide `sed -i` sweep
+- a cleanup script deleting stale temp files by pattern
+
+So before any command that applies an edit or a delete across a set of files
+it does not name one by one — `sed -i` over `find`/`xargs`/`grep -rl` output,
+`find -delete`, `find -exec`, `rm` with a glob or `-r`, `git clean` — scope it
+so `queue/` cannot be reached:
+
+```
+find . -path ./queue -prune -o -name '*.tmp' -delete
+grep -rl old . --exclude-dir=queue | xargs sed -i 's/old/new/'
+```
+
+Naming the files explicitly works too. Reading `queue/` is fine; only writes
+and deletes are dangerous. If a task genuinely requires modifying `queue/`,
+stop and ask — do not proceed on your own judgement.
+
+`.claude/hooks/guard-queue.py` refuses these command shapes as a backstop
+(`python3 .claude/hooks/test_guard.py` covers what it does and does not catch).
+It reads the command text only, so it cannot see inside a script you invoke by
+path, and it does not know what a Python or Node one-liner will unlink. It is a
+net under the rule above, not a substitute for it.
+
